In the Middle East, revenues from fossil fuel exports constitute 68\% of total fiscal income \cite{WorldBank2023MENAUpdate,FATTOUH2013108}, and their economies are highly dependent on the stable cash flow generated by oil and gas trade \cite{karanfil2023energy}. For example, Saudi Arabia secures its revenue through long-term export contracts, which account for 82\% of its trade volume \cite{GECF2023AGMR}. Additionally, it leverages sovereign wealth funds, such as the Public Investment Fund (PIF) with assets exceeding \$700 billion, to directly invest in renewable energy projects, thereby mitigating market financing risks. 76\% of green hydrogen and photovoltaic projects in the Middle East are financed through sovereign guarantees \cite{IRENA2023Statistics}, resulting in a WACC with a risk premium of only 1.2\%, significantly lower than that in Europe (3.5\%). This ``fiscal cushion'' ensures the rigidity of capital costs; even if international oil prices fluctuate from \$80 per barrel to \$60 per barrel, the WACC for Saudi photovoltaic projects varies by merely 0.3\% \cite{SPGlobal2024GCCInfrastructureOutlook}.

\subsubsection{Institutional Design}

Middle Eastern governments use a three-tier framework to reduce the impact of fossil fuel price volatility on energy transition costs \cite{surkov2023middle}, creating a ``policy isolation layer.''
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Dual-track system: Low domestic energy prices (Saudi gasoline at \$0.6 per liter, one-third of U.S. rates) suppress alternative energy demand \cite{fattouh2013energy}. Subsidies enhance renewable energy competitiveness; for instance, Gulf countries' fossil fuel subsidies account for 8.2\% of GDP \cite{IMF2023FossilFuelSubsidies}. Long-term agreements, like Dubai Solar Park's 20-year fixed electricity price (4.5 cents/kWh), decouple capital costs from short-term price fluctuations.
    \item Strategic reserves and production adjustments: Middle East oil reserves cover 214 days of demand \cite{IMF2023FossilFuelSubsidies}. Governments stabilize prices by adjusting production.
    \item Sovereign guaranties: Using the Barakah nuclear power plant in the United Arab Emirates as an example, 90\% of its funding originates from state-owned banks, maintaining a stable debt cost of 3.2\%. The correlation coefficient $R^2$ between its financing costs and oil price fluctuations is only 0.02 \cite{OECDNEA2019LTO}.
\end{enumerate}

